question:
A grocery app exports orders as nested JSON. Each order has a list of items, and an order can have an empty list. Flatten the data so there is one line per item with `order_id`, `sku` and `qty`. Assign to `result` a dict with three keys: `lines` (number of item lines), `sum_qty` (total quantity across all lines) and `orders_without_items` (number of orders with an empty item list).

```
{"orders": [
 {"order_id": "A1", "items": [{"sku": "TEA-500", "qty": 2}, {"sku": "BIS-200", "qty": 1}]},
 {"order_id": "A2", "items": []},
 {"order_id": "A3", "items": [{"sku": "SOAP-75", "qty": 4}]}
]}
```

solutionCode:
import json

raw = '{"orders": [{"order_id": "A1", "items": [{"sku": "TEA-500", "qty": 2}, {"sku": "BIS-200", "qty": 1}]}, {"order_id": "A2", "items": []}, {"order_id": "A3", "items": [{"sku": "SOAP-75", "qty": 4}]}]}'
data = json.loads(raw)
lines = []
empty = 0
for order in data["orders"]:
    if not order["items"]:
        empty += 1
    for item in order["items"]:
        lines.append((order["order_id"], item["sku"], item["qty"]))
result = {"lines": len(lines), "sum_qty": sum(q for _, _, q in lines), "orders_without_items": empty}
